<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs exclusions for {{MODULE_SLUG}}.

  Small on purpose. An exclusion list that starts full is a list nobody ever
  shortens, and every entry in it is a bug class the team agreed to stop seeing.
  Each entry below names the finding, says why it is safe *here*, and says what
  would make it unsafe again — and each is expected to be removed when that reason
  goes away. A finding that is not listed here must be fixed, not suppressed.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>
  <!--
    EI_EXPOSE_REP2 — "may expose internal representation by storing an externally
    mutable object into a field".

    The real finding here is that the pattern SpotBugs is warning about is how
    Spring does dependency injection: a constructor takes a collaborator, and a
    field holds it for the object's lifetime. Suppressing that would mean
    suppressing injection, so the finding is addressed per-class instead.

    A service or an SDK client injected this way is shared, effectively immutable
    for the lifetime of the application, and never handed to a caller who could
    mutate it. The field is `final` and private, and nothing exposes it: there is
    no getter and no public accessor, so there is no path from a caller to the
    stored object.

    This becomes a real finding the moment any of the following is true:
      - a setter or any other write path to the field is added,
      - a getter returns the field directly rather than a copy,
      - the injected type is mutable per-request state rather than a shared
        stateless collaborator,
      - the class stops being a Spring bean and is constructed by hand, where a
        caller genuinely can retain a reference it was given.
    In those cases delete this entry and fix the code instead.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2" />
    <Or>
      <Class name="~.*\.\w*Controller" />
      <Class name="~.*\.\w*Service" />
    </Or>
    <Or>
      <Method name="&lt;init&gt;" />
    </Or>
  </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
